        "id": 28,
        "unit": "III",
        "question": "State the Kapustinskii equation for lattice energy and calculate the lattice energy ($\\Delta H_L$) of $\\text{NaCl}$ using a Born-Haber cycle with the following data (all values in $\\text{kJ/mol}$): $ \\Delta H_f^\\circ = -411.2, \\quad \\Delta H_{\\text{sub}} = 108.4, \\quad \\text{IE} = 495.4, \\quad \\Delta H_{\\text{diss}}(\\text{Cl}_2) = 241.8, \\quad \\text{EA}(\\text{Cl}) = -348.6 $",
        "answerKey": "1. **Born-Haber Cycle Concept**: - The Born-Haber cycle is a thermodynamic cycle that relates the lattice energy of an ionic crystal to other thermochemical data such as ionization energy, electron affinity, dissociation energy, sublimation energy, and enthalpy of formation. - It is an application of **Hess's Law**. 2. **Steps for NaCl Lattice Energy**: - Sublimation of solid sodium: $\\text{Na(s)} \\to \\text{Na(g)} \\quad (\\Delta H_{\\text{sub}})$ - Dissociation of chlorine molecules: $\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Cl}_2\\text{(g)} \\to \\text{Cl(g)} \\quad (\\frac{1}{2}D)$ - Ionization of gaseous sodium atoms: $\\text{Na(g)} \\to \\text{Na}^+\\text{(g)} + \\text{e}^- \\quad (IE)$ - Electron gain by gaseous chlorine atoms: $\\text{Cl(g)} + \\text{e}^- \\to \\text{Cl}^-\\text{(g)} \\quad (EA)$ - Combination of gaseous ions to form solid crystal: $\\text{Na}^+\\text{(g)} + \\text{Cl}^-\\text{(g)} \\to \\text{NaCl(s)} \\quad (U_{\\text{lattice}})$ 3. **Mathematical Formula**: - By Hess's law: $$\\Delta H_f = \\Delta H_{\\text{sub}} + \\frac{1}{2}D + IE + EA + U_{\\text{lattice}}$$ - Rearranging this allows calculations of the lattice energy ($U_{\\text{lattice}}$), which is highly exothermic."
