        "unit": "I",
        "question": "Discuss the structure and bonding in diborane ($\\text{B}_2\\text{H}_6$).",
        "answerKey": "1. **Structure of Diborane ($\\text{B}_2\\text{H}_6$)**: - Diborane contains twelve valence electrons, which is insufficient to form standard 2-centre-2-electron covalent bonds between all atoms (electron-deficient molecule). - It has two types of hydrogen atoms: four terminal hydrogens (in plane with boron atoms) and two bridging hydrogens (above and below the plane). 2. **Bonding and Banana Bonds**: - The four terminal $\\text{B}-\\text{H}$ bonds are normal 2-centre-2-electron (2c-2e) covalent bonds formed by $\\text{sp}^3$ hybridized boron orbitals and $1\\text{s}$ hydrogen orbitals. - The two bridging $\\text{B}-\\text{H}-\\text{B}$ bonds are 3-centre-2-electron (3c-2e) bonds, often called **banana bonds**. In these bonds, a pair of electrons is shared among three nuclei (two Borons and one Hydrogen). 3. **Physical Properties and Confirmation**: - Hybridization of boron is $\\text{sp}^3$. - The terminal $\\text{B}-\\text{H}$ bond length is shorter ($1.19\\text{ \\u00c5}$) compared to the bridging $\\text{B}-\\text{H}$ bond length ($1.33\\text{ \\u00c5}$), confirming the weaker and multi-centre nature of the bridge bonds."
