        "question": "Discuss the following reactions along with mechanisms: (1) Beckmann rearrangement (2) Baeyer-Villiger oxidation",
        "answerKey": "1. **Beckmann Rearrangement Concept**: - The Beckmann rearrangement is the acid-catalyzed conversion of an **oxime** (usually ketoxime) into an **N-substituted amide**. - Catalysts used include $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$, $\\text{PCl}_5$, $\\text{SOCl}_2$, or polyphosphoric acid (PPA). 2. **Reaction Mechanism**: - **Protonation**: The hydroxyl group of the ketoxime is protonated to form a good leaving group ($-\\text{OH}_2^+$). - **Migration and Elimination**: Water is eliminated with simultaneous migration of the alkyl/aryl group that is **anti (trans)** to the leaving hydroxyl group to the nitrogen atom, forming an iminocarbocation. - **Nucleophilic Attack**: Water attacks the iminocarbocation, followed by deprotonation to yield an imidic acid intermediate. - **Tautomerization**: The imidic acid tautomerizes to the more stable amide (keto-enol type tautomerism). 3. **Application**: - A major industrial application is the rearrangement of cyclohexanone oxime to **caprolactam**, which is the monomer used for synthesis of Nylon-6."
