        "answerKey": "1. **Skraup Synthesis Overview**: - The Skraup synthesis is a classical method for preparing **quinoline** by heating aniline with glycerol, concentrated sulfuric acid (dehydrating agent and catalyst), and an oxidizing agent (typically nitrobenzene or arsenic pentoxide). 2. **Stepwise Reaction Mechanism**: - **Dehydration**: Glycerol undergoes acid-catalyzed dehydration by $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$ to form **acrolein** (propenal), an $\\alpha,\\beta$-unsaturated aldehyde. - **1,4-Addition**: Aniline acts as a nucleophile and undergoes Michael-type (1,4-addition) conjugate addition to the acrolein, forming $\\beta$-phenylaminopropionaldehyde. - **Ring Closure**: The intermediate undergoes acid-catalyzed intramolecular electrophilic cyclization (similar to Friedel-Crafts alkylation) onto the ortho-position of the aromatic ring, followed by dehydration to yield 1,2-dihydroquinoline. - **Oxidation**: The 1,2-dihydroquinoline is oxidized by nitrobenzene (or $\\text{As}_2\\text{O}_5$) to yield **quinoline**."
