        "unit": "II",
        "question": "Describe the construction and reactions of the nickel-cadmium accumulator.",
        "answerKey": "1. **Lead Storage Battery Overview**: - The lead-acid storage battery is a secondary cell (rechargeable battery) consisting of a Lead ($Pb$) anode, a Lead dioxide ($PbO_2$) cathode, and aqueous sulfuric acid ($38\\% \\text{ w/w } \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$, density $1.28\\text{ g/cm}^3$) as the electrolyte. 2. **Discharging Reactions (anode/cathode)**: - **At Anode**: Lead metal is oxidized to lead sulfate: $$\\text{Pb(s)} + \\text{SO}_4^{2-}\\text{(aq)} \\to \\text{PbSO}_4\\text{(s)} + 2\\text{e}^-$$ - **At Cathode**: Lead dioxide is reduced to lead sulfate: $$\\text{PbO}_2\\text{(s)} + \\text{SO}_4^{2-}\\text{(aq)} + 4\\text{H}^+\\text{(aq)} + 2\\text{e}^- \\to \\text{PbSO}_4\\text{(s)} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O(l)}$$ - **Net Discharging Reaction**: $$\\text{Pb(s)} + \\text{PbO}_2\\text{(s)} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4\\text{(aq)} \\to 2\\text{PbSO}_4\\text{(s)} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O(l)}$$ Lead sulfate precipitates on both electrodes, and sulfuric acid is consumed. 3. **Recharging Reactions**: - During charging, the reactions are reversed by applying an external electrical potential. Lead sulfate on the anode is reduced back to lead metal, and lead sulfate on the cathode is oxidized back to lead dioxide, regenerating $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$."
